  "cause": "This entry is an update, and the update moves the war one level up the chain of command. **Iran has \"substantially increased efforts to target US personnel\" in the Middle East and other areas, the US national intelligence director warned in a memo seen by the BBC — marked unclassified and sent to Secretary of State Marco Rubio on Friday. \"The lethal plotting efforts by the Iranians against US citizens have reached advanced stages in both the Middle East and certain other countries,\" Jay Clayton wrote. The assessment comes as the State Department adjusts staffing at diplomatic posts in the region, including the ordered departure earlier this week of non-emergency embassy staff from Saudi Arabia — a response to escalating Houthi attacks on the kingdom in the past month** (BBC). **A US official: \"The threat of Houthi attacks and of Iranian terror plots targeting US officials are informing our decisions on staffing adjustments for embassies on [Restricted Operations] Status in the region\"** (BBC). **The military ledger has thickened on both sides since this page last counted it.** **At least 21 people have now been killed in Houthi attacks on Saudi airports since the start of October, according to Saudi authorities; the 10 October missile strike on Riyadh's international airport killed 13 and injured 308 — the deadliest attack in Saudi Arabia in more than a decade. The Houthis call the attacks retaliation for what they call a Saudi blockade of Yemeni ports and airports and for a strike on Sanaa airport they blame on the kingdom. Some 155 civilians have been killed in Yemen since July, per the Houthi-run health ministry; more than 70 security personnel were killed this week in a missile strike on al-Alam camp near Ataq in Shabwa, and the same ministry says Saudi air and artillery strikes have killed at least 141 and injured 395 since 3 September. Britain is now deploying RAF Typhoons to defend Saudi cities and infrastructure — authorised by Prime Minister Andy Burnham, with Defence Secretary Wes Streeting stating the aircraft \"will operate in a defensive role and will not conduct offensive strikes\"** (BBC). **The frame is a war that never closed: the US and Israel launched an air campaign against Iran in February, killing its leader; Tehran retaliated across the region and effectively closed the Strait of Hormuz, through which about a fifth of the world's oil and gas passes; a June agreement to end the war and reopen the strait collapsed within weeks when attacks on shipping resumed and the US reinstated its blockade. Behind all of it, Taiz remains cut off and around 800,000 Yemenis have lost access to lifesaving aid, as recorded here yesterday** (BBC; UN).",
