  "verse": "Anyone who undertakes haggling (usury/greed) in any form of commerce and unrightfully demands a markup (interest or price increase) shall be exposed to the punishment of recompense through the authority or jurisdiction, so that the disadvantaged ones do not suffer any loss of goods and chattels, possessions and fortune; in commerce involving goods and things, the markup (interest or price increase) shall be regulated and defined by the authority according to the goods or things and their value…",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 2, Verse 388",
  "hinge": "The cause is not the 50% and it is not Canada. It is the choice — by the White House, whether tariff relief is granted on published criteria or on electoral geography; by Congress, whether it reclaims the authority §2 assigns to a deliberative body rather than to an announcement; by Ottawa, whether the countermeasures are published as a reversible schedule tied to US moves rather than held as a permanent domestic entitlement; and by the border-state firms themselves, whether they invest on the assumption that a favour granted can be withdrawn. §2 puts the markup question where it belongs — with the authority, regulating by value, so that the disadvantaged do not bear the loss — and by that standard a levy lifted for one product line in one Senate race is not regulation but arbitrariness wearing its clothes. Whether the next tariff list is written by a process or by a campaign is not fixed by the trade deficit; it is a decision held by named officials, named senators and named ministers, and one that a different choice could still make."
